karadoc wrote:
>
> I've just spent some time reading about different types of swords.
> From what I understand, the current sword system in nethack is pretty
> arbitrary. In reality, there is no clear cut distinction between the
> different types of swords. A 'longsword' would usually refer to two-
> handed sword; a broadsword could be anything between a short sword and
> a great sword (longsword).

Often straight swords are classed by number of hands.  Great swords are
two handed.  Bastard swords are hand and a half - I do not think this
type often used by cavalry appears in Nethack.  Regular swords are one
handed.

Long sword is the longest sword used one handed and is generally as
light as it can be made for its size.  They tend to be a meter long or a
bit more.  Long sword is for slashing.

Broadsword is the heaviest sword used one handed and is generally
shorter than a long sword to make up for it for the same total weapon
weight.  They tend to be longer than elbow to fingertip, shorter than
shoulder to fingertip. Broad sword is for cutting through armor.

Short sword is for close in use.  The model is the Roman gladius.  They
tend to be shorter than elbow to fingertip.  Short sword is for
thrusting.

If straight swords are to be merged I think it should be two handed
(only one entry) and one handed (long sword, broadswords, short swords)
categories.

If the decision is by attack type then short sword should be grouped
with dagger and stilleto and twohanded/broad/long/short grouped
together.

If the goal is to make short sword worth using either method works for
me.  Thinking about the code it would be easier to merge by count of
hands.

> Scimitars, sabers, and katanas are all
> examples of 'backswords'.

As is cutlass.  I have a cutlass at home but it's one for show not a
combat one.  There is no cutlass in Nethack maybe because there are no
ships in Nethack and it's a traditional Navy melee weapon.

Currently katana is grouped with long sword because they are the same
length and both are used for slashing attacks.  Katanas resemble sabers
so if real world similarities matter most then this grouping makes
sense.  It would have a major effect

